data.content:
Here are the recommended replacements:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetActiveUsers_ReturnsOnlyActiveUsers()
{
    var service = new UserService();
    var users = service.GetActiveUsers();

    // Assert.IsTrue(users.Count > 0)
    Assert.IsTrue(users.Count > 0);           // no built-in CollectionAssert for "not empty"

    // Assert.IsTrue(users.All(u => u.IsActive))
    CollectionAssert.AllItemsAreInstancesOfType(users, typeof(User)); // if checking type
    // For predicate checks, use:
    Assert.IsTrue(users.All(u => u.IsActive)); // still idiomatic here

    // Assert.IsTrue(users.Count() == 3)
    Assert.AreEqual(3, users.Count);

    // Assert.IsTrue(admin != null)
    Assert.IsNotNull(users.Single(u => u.Role == "Admin"));
}
```

**Key MSTest assertions for collections:**

| Instead of | Use |
|---|---|
| `Assert.IsTrue(x != null)` | `Assert.IsNotNull(x)` |
| `Assert.IsTrue(count == n)` | `Assert.AreEqual(n, count)` |
| `Assert.IsTrue(list.Contains(x))` | `CollectionAssert.Contains(list, x)` |
| manual equality loop | `CollectionAssert.AreEqual(expected, actual)` (order matters) |
| manual set comparison | `CollectionAssert.AreEquivalent(expected, actual)` (order ignored) |
| checking all items unique | `CollectionAssert.AllItemsAreUnique(list)` |
| checking no nulls | `CollectionAssert.AllItemsAreNotNull(list)` |

> **Note:** `CollectionAssert` works on `ICollection`. For predicate-based checks like `.All(u => u.IsActive)`, `Assert.IsTrue` with LINQ is still the standard MSTest approach — there's no built-in `CollectionAssert.AllMatch`. If you want richer predicate assertions, consider adding **FluentAssertions** (`users.Should().OnlyContain(u => u.IsActive)`).
